<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/2nd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cimbrian War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">113–101 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cimbrian War was fought from 113 to 101 BCE between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> and the migrating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbri">Cimbri</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutones">Teutones</a>. The tribes inflicted several defeats on Roman armies, worst at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-vaucluse">Arausio</a> in 105 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-marius">Gaius Marius</a>, repeatedly elected consul, reformed the army and destroyed the Teutones at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aix-en-provence">Aquae Sextiae</a> in 102 BCE and the Cimbri at Vercellae in 101 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cimbrian-war.jpg" alt="Cimbrian War" width="320" height="272" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pethrus · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cimbrians_and_Teutons_invasions.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbri">Cimbri</a> were Germanic and Celtic tribes who, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutones">Teutones</a>, Ambrones and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigurini">Tigurini</a>, migrated from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> peninsula into Roman-controlled territory. According to some Roman accounts the Cimbri left their lands around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> because of flooding, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> thought this unlikely. The Teutones joined them, and in 113 BCE they reached Noricum on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, home to the Roman-allied Taurisci, who asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> for aid. The war was the first time since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a> that Italia and Rome itself had been seriously threatened.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The following year the consul Gnaeus Papirius Carbo demanded that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbri">Cimbri</a> leave Noricum. They began to comply but found that Carbo had laid an ambush, and at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noreia">Noreia</a> they annihilated his army. They then moved west into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and defeated Roman armies in 109 and 107 BCE, the latter at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Burdigala</a>, where the consul Lucius Cassius Longinus Ravalla was killed.</p><p>In 105 BCE the consul Gnaeus Mallius Maximus and the proconsul Quintus Servilius Caepio gathered more than 80,000 men. They distrusted each other and camped on opposite banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> near Orange. Caepio attacked without support and his legions were wiped out, after which Maximus's troops were defeated too. Only a few hundred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> escaped. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-vaucluse">Arausio</a> was the costliest defeat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> had suffered since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Cannae</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-marius">Gaius Marius</a> was elected consul for five years running from 104 BCE and rebuilt the army. In 102 BCE he routed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutones">Teutones</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aix-en-provence">Aquae Sextiae</a>, using five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohorts</a> hidden in a wood, and captured their king, Teutobod. In 101 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbri">Cimbri</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> into northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and Marius and Quintus Lutatius Catulus defeated them at Vercellae. Their leaders Boiorix and Lugius fell, and the women killed themselves and their children to avoid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Some surviving captives are reported to have been among the gladiators in the Third Servile War. The war marked the start of the rivalry between Marius and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a>, which led to the first of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s great civil wars. After Vercellae Marius granted Roman citizenship to two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohorts</a> of allied Italian soldiers without asking the Senate. According to traditional historiography the Italian allies then demanded a larger say in Roman policy, leading to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce">Social War</a>. The putative Marian reforms of the legions are now contested by modern historians.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cimbrian War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was Rome's worst defeat of the <b>Cimbrian War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-6">6 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
